From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Wed, 26 Oct 1994 02:47:09 -0500
Subject: [#94-10-552] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

At 01:16 PM 10/26/94, schooley@new-orleans.neosoft.com (John Schooley) wrote:
>>>>Michael Price
[consciousness as modelling with self-reference]
>>>Steve Witham:
[consciousness is the ability to make up a first-person story 
that jibes reasonably with what one does per Dennet]
>
>Dennett's idea is not unlike Zen Buddhist ideas except that they would
>claim that Dennet's first person story indicates that the first person is
>not apriori.  In other words consciousness can functionally exist even if
>the first person story is dismantled or dropped.[1]  [...]

This is an interesting stack of definitions. Zen training is supposed
to allow you to function without the crutch of constructing the
first-person story all the time, as if you were justifying yourself
to yourself, or more likely to mental agents who represent those who
might soon or have in the past asked you for your story. Then of course
you find you can pick up the "story" at any time (not that I ever
got that far with Zen).

One might get to the point where personalities were tools, that is,
you pick up the story that fits the situation. We can see a hint of
this in actors and MPD cases (many of them on Usenet :-) already. This
is where our concepts of formal reputations come in real handy, as we
would not be able to depend on an assumed consistency of personality,
or indeed on our hypotheses about another's purposes, without
authentication. 

In short, uploads must protect their private crypto keys, or they're
dead. Hmmm. SF story possibility.

[1] Back to this point for a second -- you often hear the objection
that consciousness must be "of" something; I prefer the simple
term "attention" for what is meant by this aspect of mental function,
which is like a tuning knob for consciousness.

