From: "Kobielus, James" <KobielJ@usva8.dyncorp.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Oct 94 17:13:00 PDT
Subject: [#94-10-554] Anti-Statism Not Consistent with Boundless Expansion

Boundless expansion--throughout outer and inner space--seems to call for a 
strong, enduring, well-heeled megastate, megacorporation, and/or 
megareligion supported by all humanity.

OUTER SPACE:  Extropians have ambitious plans to probe, explore, colonize, 
terraform, cultivate, and exploit all resources in the solar system for 
human ends.  Realizing this goal would require a bureaucratic apparatus that 
can enlist all nations, execute plans, mobilize resources, sponsor R&D, 
construct monstruous public works, and launch and support interplanetary 
missions over many generations.

INNER SPACE:  Extropians desire to achieve physical/spiritual immortality 
through cryogenics, cloning, and neural replication; elevate humanity to the 
next evolutionary plateau through eugenics and mosaic conception/birthing; 
overcome organic limitations through robotics and genetic engineering; 
extend our minds and senses through information technology; and 
microminiaturize the arts and sciences through nanotechnology.  Every one of 
these goals would require considerable long-term, global R&D and would 
inflict wrenching changes on ideologies, religions, legal systems, and codes 
of interpersonal conduct everywhere.

Anybody who believes that these utopian goals can be achieved through pure 
laissez-fair market mechanics without prodding/direction from central or 
decentralized authorities should explain how.  None of this stuff will 
spontaneously emerge from the primordial ooze.

Every single Extropian dream requires massive infrastructure development and 
a state apparatus unlike any in human experience.  Scattered ideamongers, 
code cutters, and gene splicers won't make it all happen.

Jim Kobielus

