Subject: [#94-10-555] BABY: Reproduction

John Schooley, writing to Nancy Lebovitz said:
>Parents have been designing their offspring for a very long time
>through mate selection.  [...] They needed to choose a genetically
>superior [mate...]

  As I understand genetics, until recently genes weren't visible (or
even concieved of).  Choseing mates based on genotype was and is
impractical.  We are still forced to base selections on phenotype, not
genotype, for almost all features.  And, of course, there is no way to
know before hand which genes will be (or even tend to be) successfull
ones.

  We can't today select the genes for financial success, running
ability, or even nose size.  Although I understand that we do have a
prety good handle on gender, and a few inherited disorders.  We can
select for those mates who have a particular gender, financial
success, running ability, or even nose size.  But this gives us only
limited control over a baby's actual genotype or phenotype.

