From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 94 17:03:00 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-556] BABY: Reproduction

Nancy Lebovitz writes (among other things):

> Part of this may be
>an assumption that my genetic traits will always be bundled
>together, so that I will never have an option about my height.
>Damned if I know how I'd feel about it if it were some trait
>like diabetes that's clearly a disease.

A close friend of mine has diabetes, and has actually told me that he 
*loves* it.  He's one of the most alive (and, interestingly, one of the most 
*extropian*) people I know, and I'm convinced (and he agrees) that this his 
largely due to the disease.  This subtle yet daily reminder of his mortality 
makes him adventurous, ambitious, and desirous of living life to the 
fullest.  At the same time, the disease requires him to discipline himself 
in order to feel well, so he eats better, exercises more, etc. than anyone 
else I know.

>The future I'd like to see is one where people are choosing a
>wild variety of physical types for all sorts of reasons.....
>and where furniture and the like is adaptable to different
>sized people.

Would anyone *choose* to be diabetic (or choose to have diabetic offspring)? 
 I doubt it.  But here's a case where a condition which probably would have 
been screened out before birth as a bad thing actually enhances the life of 
the "victim".  If we were to eliminate all "imperfections" such as shortness 
or mild diabetes before birth, I feel we might be creating people who are 
less than what they would have been had they overcome their genetic 
"obstacle".


Steven Webb
webbs@oplc.psb.bls.gov


