From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 94 17:59:50 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-558] Astrology and CSICOP/_Skeptical Inquirer_ 

Edgar W. Swank describes a speculative justification for astrology:

>I read one speculation that a possible mechanism of influence of
>planet positions would be tidal forces on the sun, which could alter
>the sunspot cycle and the amount of radiation hitting the earth. This
>could certainly affect human lives by chaotic effects on weather &
>climate, floods, droughts, etc.

It is possible to construct elaborate and sometimes humorous explanations for
the ``validity'' of astrology.  But why stop there.  Many other long standing
``disciplines'' are waiting for attempts at rationalization.  For example:

1) Divination of the future by examining the entrails of a sacrificed
chicken.

2) Prediction of the future by looking at tea leave patterns.

3) Insight into the character and mental capacity of an individual by
studying the bumps on a persons head, i.e., phrenology

These methods are currently not as popular as astrology.  But they have been
very popular in the past and the phenomenon of popularity can exhibit an
intriguing cyclicity.  After constructing your theory you should also
consider the following alternative/complementary theory:  The main reason
these disciplines were invented was to allow the manipulation of the
gullible by charlatans for monetary gain.       gfs

